{"course":"g9-mus","title":"Music I: Theory & Performance","units":[{"t":"Notation Basics: Staff, Clefs and Pitch","lessons":[{"t":"The Grand Staff","learn":["Music is notated on a five-line staff; the grand staff joins treble and bass clefs with a brace.","The treble (G) clef curls around G above middle C; the bass (F) clef's dots surround F below middle C.","The alto (C) clef, used by violas, centres on middle C."],"check":[["The treble clef is also called the ___ clef.","G","F","C"],["The bass clef's dots surround...","F","G","C"],["The alto clef is used by...","Violas","Flutes","Tubas"]],"try":"Write middle C in treble, bass and alto clefs."},{"t":"Pitch Names and Octaves","learn":["Pitches repeat in octaves; scientific pitch notation names middle C as C4.","A4 vibrates at 440 Hz, the standard tuning pitch.","Each octave up doubles the frequency."],"check":[["Middle C is...","C4","C1","C8"],["A4 vibrates at...","440 Hz","100 Hz","1,000 Hz"],["An octave up ___ the frequency.","Doubles","Halves","Keeps"]],"try":"Find C3, C4 and C5 on a keyboard."},{"t":"Accidentals and Enharmonics","learn":["Sharps raise a half step, flats lower, naturals cancel; double sharps and double flats move two half steps.","Enharmonic equivalents sound the same: F♯ and G♭.","Accidentals apply for the rest of the measure."],"check":[["A double sharp raises a note...","Two half steps","One half step","An octave"],["F♯ is enharmonic with...","G♭","F♭","E♯"],["Accidentals last until...","The bar line","The end of the piece","One note"]],"try":"Write five enharmonic pairs."},{"t":"Tenor Clef","learn":["A C clef on the fourth line.","Used by cello, bassoon and trombone in high passages.","Avoids many ledger lines."],"check":[["Tenor clef puts middle C on the ___ line.","Fourth","Third","First"],["Cellos use tenor clef in ___ passages.","High","Low","Silent"],["It avoids many...","Ledger lines","Rests","Bars"]],"try":"Read ten tenor clef notes."},{"t":"Scientific Pitch Notation","learn":["Middle